+\textbf{Leaked generator reasoning.}\label{sec:leak} Not every output is a clean story. In 84 of the 900 (9.3\%), the generator's planning leaked into the text, which opens with lines such as ``The user wants a paragraph about\ldots''. A case-insensitive pattern finds them: \texttt{<analysis>|</?think|The user wants|the user (is asking|wants)|key constraint}. The pattern undercounts. It misses planning that opens ``I need to'' or ``Thinking Process'', a few refusals, and scaffolding such as a verification checklist. A broader pattern, written after reading all 900 outputs and before computing anything on it, marks 141 (15.7\%). They cluster by topic: 22 of the 84 are astronaut stories, and none are scientist or doctor stories. Seventy-five outputs contain ``deeply'' followed by their own label; 43 of them are among the 84, and 73 among the 141. Most probes do worse on these outputs, so the leak does not inflate the results. On the 84, text (TF-IDF) scores 0.226 against 0.398 on the other 816, the cache probe (R2) 0.286 against 0.365, and the v2 code path (R1) 0.202 against 0.431; the exploratory residual-stream LR is about level (0.512 against 0.493). With the 84 set aside, R2 is 3.3 points below text (TF-IDF) (0.365 against 0.398); with all 141 set aside, it is 2.6 points below (0.383 against 0.410). These splits are descriptive. No split was preregistered, and the registered tests ran on all 900. \texttt{leak\_split.py} in \texttt{correction\_v2/} computes every number in this paragraph from the released stories and results files.
+
 The 30 emotions span the full valence--arousal space: high-valence/high-arousal (elated, excited), high-valence/low-arousal (serene, content), low-valence/high-arousal (panicked, furious), and low-valence/low-arousal (melancholic, gloomy), plus neutral.
